                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the design failure`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Your “senior assistance lamp” — oversized buttons, an emergency button — just scored a disaster: in a 90-person panel of 60–75-year-olds, <strong>71% said it made them feel old</strong> and <strong>8% purchase intent</strong> against a 25% hurdle. The product <em>works</em>; nobody wants it. Diagnose <em>why</em>: this is a failure of <strong>design management and meaning</strong>, not engineering — the lamp solved a functional problem while signalling stigma. Name what “good design for longevity” must lead with, and the principles the lamp violated.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the segment`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The over-60 market is a <strong>€90M</strong> serviceable opportunity — but it is not one segment. <strong>Active Agers (60–70)</strong> are healthy and self-image-driven; <strong>Supported Living (75+)</strong> is function- and safety-driven. They want opposite things, and designing for both at once pleases neither — a healthy 63-year-old sees a hospital product, a frail 80-year-old finds it too complex. <em>Choose.</em> Pick a target, frame the value proposition around its real job-to-be-done, and focus the line rather than averaging across needs.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Integrate design & balance the three value axes`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Make the central design-management call: <strong>true inclusive/universal design with co-design</strong> (adjustable, dignified, beautiful — <strong>+€1.1M</strong> in design and tooling) versus a <strong>cosmetic re-skin</strong> — the option that just scored 8%. Then allocate the <strong>€4.0M</strong> across the three value axes: design depth, societal value (co-design, accessibility, repairability/take-back), and business value (pricing, channel, marketing). Set a price that keeps payback inside the CFO’s <em>30-month hurdle</em>. Show how design integration creates desirability <em>and</em> social value rather than trading them off.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Commit the launch`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Lock the design, segment, price, channel and the social-impact commitments, and take the go/no-go to the board. Show concretely <strong>where design actually changed the product, the experience or the business model</strong> — not a marketing launch with a nicer logo. Defend how the line creates <strong>personal, business AND societal value at once</strong>, and why it beats the Scandinavian “dignified-ageing” rival on <em>meaning, not features</em>. A profitable product that betrays the brief, or a beautiful one that loses money for three years, both fail. Make it pay <em>and</em> mean something.`,

