                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_meaning:       `<strong>That’s the right diagnosis.</strong> The lamp <em>worked</em> — and 71% still said it made them feel old. The failure is meaning, not engineering: the product solved a functional problem while signalling stigma. Now design for what it <em>says</em> about the person who buys it.`,
                    coach_r1_features:      `<strong>You made the emergency button bigger and added fall detection — and 71% still feel old.</strong> The product works; nobody wants it. This isn’t an engineering failure, it’s a design-and-meaning failure. What is the product <em>saying</em> to the person who buys it?`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>Price wasn’t the problem — meaning was.</strong> Testers didn’t say “too expensive,” they said “it makes me feel old.” A discount on a stigmatising object is still a stigmatising object. Fix what it signals first.`,
                    coach_r1_awareness:     `<strong>Better messaging won’t rescue the wrong meaning.</strong> The panel understood the lamp perfectly — that’s exactly why they rejected it. You can’t market your way out of a product that tells people they’re frail.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as a meaning failure <em>and</em> led with dignity, desirability and usability. That is design-for-longevity done right — function without stigma, self-image first. The whole redesign now has a north star.`,
                    coach_r1_safety:        `<strong>Leading with safety and monitoring is how you rebuild the lamp that already failed.</strong> Protective features signal frailty; that’s the stigma testers rejected. Dignity and desirability come first — function without making the buyer feel old.`,
                    coach_r1_tech:          `<strong>Sensors, apps and remote monitoring read as surveillance, not design.</strong> An Active Ager doesn’t want to be tracked; they want a beautiful object that fits their life. Lead with meaning, not connectivity.`,
                    coach_r1_lowconviction: `<strong>If you won’t commit the board to the meaning diagnosis, they’ll default to features.</strong> Half-hearted framing invites another beige assistance product. Hold the line: this is a design-and-meaning failure.`,
                    coach_r2_active:        `<strong>Active Agers is a disciplined, identity-driven choice.</strong> Healthy 60–70s with willingness-to-pay want to live well and feel like themselves — design and desirability are exactly your strengths. Now frame the value proposition around that job, not assistance.`,
                    coach_r2_supported:     `<strong>Supported Living is a legitimate, function-led target</strong> — but it’s the harder fit for an award-winning design house, and dignity still matters: even a safety product must not stigmatise. Keep desirability in the brief, not just protection.`,
                    coach_r2_staged:        `<strong>A staged play can work if the roadmap is genuinely coherent.</strong> Design-led for Active Agers now, extend to Supported Living later — one line, sequenced, not averaged. Just don’t let “later” become “both at once.”`,
                    coach_r2_both:          `<strong>You’re designing one line for a stylish 63-year-old and a frail 80-year-old.</strong> The 63-year-old sees a hospital product; the 80-year-old finds it too complex. Who exactly is this for? Serving everyone in the silver economy serves no one. Choose.`,
                    coach_r2_medical:       `<strong>A clinical/medical positioning for Active Agers is the stigma trap again.</strong> Healthy 60-somethings don’t want a care device — they want a beautiful object. Medical framing is precisely what scored 8%.`,
                    coach_r2_mismatch:      `<strong>“Stay safe and assisted” is the wrong job for an Active Ager.</strong> Their job-to-be-done is to live well and feel like themselves, not to be protected. Match the proposition to the identity, or you re-create the lamp.`,
                    coach_r2_lowfocus:      `<strong>Low focus means you’re still averaging across needs.</strong> A line that hedges between identity and assistance reads as confused to both. Commit to one segment’s job and design the whole line around it.`,
                    coach_r3_inclusive:     `<strong>True inclusive design is the decision that earns the “design-led” claim.</strong> Adjustable, dignified and beautiful — with co-design — is what turns an over-60 product from medical-beige into something people actually want. The €1.1M cliff is the price of fixing a meaning problem properly.`,
                    coach_r3_reskin:        `<strong>You saved €1.1M by re-skinning instead of redesigning.</strong> That’s almost exactly the option that scored 8% purchase intent last month. What makes you think a new colour fixes a meaning problem? You’ve repeated the failure.`,
                    coach_r3_hybrid:        `<strong>Selective inclusive details are a defensible middle ground</strong> — but a partial redesign carries partial risk. The touchpoints you didn’t rethink are where the old stigma leaks back in. Be sure the half you funded is the half that carries the meaning.`,
                    coach_r3_nosocial:      `<strong>You stripped the co-design and take-back budget to almost nothing.</strong> The longevity brief — and the brand — is about value for the user and society too. Did you just build a product that betrays the reason you entered this market? Co-design is also what makes it desirable.`,
                    coach_r3_nobusiness:    `<strong>Almost no business spend means no channel, no launch and no payback.</strong> Lumière is a €38M company, not a foundation. A beautiful, dignified product nobody can buy doesn’t create societal value — it gets cancelled. Fund the engine.`,
                    coach_r3_lowprice:      `<strong>€149 to undercut the rival blows the payback.</strong> Thin margins on a design-led product stretch recovery well past the 30-month hurdle. You don’t win the silver economy on price — you win on meaning. Price for the value you built.`,
                    coach_r4_product:       `<strong>That is what “design-led” actually looks like.</strong> Design changed the product, the experience <em>and</em> the take-back business model — visible everywhere, not bolted on. This is the version the board can defend and the rival can’t easily copy.`,
                    coach_r4_surface:       `<strong>If design only lives in the marketing, it isn’t design-led.</strong> The product is still standard; you’ve dressed it up. The board — and the older co-designers — will see the gap between the claim and the object.`,
                    coach_r4_claim:         `<strong>You keep saying this is design-led, but the plan reads like a normal launch with a nicer logo.</strong> Show me where design actually changed the product, the experience or the business model. A claim you can’t point to is the one I’ll challenge first.`,
                    coach_r4_reinforce:     `<strong>That’s the heart of the longevity brief.</strong> Co-design and dignity aren’t a cost you trade against profit — they’re what make the product desirable and therefore profitable. You’ve shown the three value axes reinforcing, not competing. That’s the win.`,
                    coach_r4_profit:        `<strong>You cut the co-design and take-back to hit payback.</strong> But the brief — and the brand — is about value for the user and society too. You may have built a profitable product that betrays the reason you entered this market. The societal axis is not optional CSR.`,
                    coach_r4_impact:        `<strong>This is a beautiful, dignified, fully-circular product that loses money for three years.</strong> Lumière is a €38M company, not a foundation. How does societal value survive if the line gets cancelled? Make it pay — that’s how the good gets to last.`,
                    coach_r4_rivalfeatures: `<strong>Out-featuring the Scandinavian rival is a losing game.</strong> Features get copied; meaning doesn’t. If your edge is “more sensors,” you’ve conceded the design argument. Win on what your design <em>says</em>, not on the spec sheet.`,
                    coach_r4_rivalprice:    `<strong>A price war against a dignified-ageing rival erodes the very margin that funds your design.</strong> You defined the category on meaning — don’t surrender it to undercut on cost and blow your payback in the process.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed meaning, not features; led with dignity.`,
