                    insight_win:            `Design-led longevity win: you diagnosed the failure as meaning, chose one identity-driven segment, funded true inclusive design over a cosmetic re-skin, and balanced the three value axes so they reinforced each other. The line is desirable, viable and dignified — and it’s the design, not the features, that the Scandinavian rival can’t copy.`,
                    insight_reskin:         `You re-skinned instead of redesigning — and a new colour doesn’t fix a meaning problem. This is the €149-or-beige path that scored 8% the first time. Saving the €1.1M was the most expensive decision in the cycle.`,
                    insight_everyone:       `You tried to serve Active Agers and Supported Living with one line and satisfied neither — the stylish 63-year-old saw a hospital product, the frail 80-year-old found it too complex. Segment discipline, not market reach, is what wins the silver economy.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn’t fully reconcile design, segment and the three value axes. The plan is closer to a meaningful launch than the failed lamp — but the seams between desirability, viability and dignity still show.`,
                    insight_undesirable:    `Desirability never cleared the 25% hurdle. Whatever else you did, older buyers still wouldn’t choose the product — the meaning problem the lamp exposed was never truly solved.`,
                    insight_desirable:      `Desirability cleared the hurdle comfortably: older buyers want this product, not because it assists them but because it fits who they are. That is the disclosure-of-self that drives purchase in the silver economy.`,
                    insight_payback:        `Payback ran past the CFO’s 30-month hurdle. A line that can’t pay back doesn’t survive at a €38M design house — societal value only lasts if the product does. Re-price for the value you built, or fund the channel that sells it.`,
                    insight_lowsocial:      `Societal value stayed thin — co-design, accessibility and take-back were under-funded or cut. That’s the CSR-bolt-on error: the longevity brief treats social value as part of the product economics, not an afterthought.`,
                    insight_claimonly:      `“Design-led” stayed a claim, not a fact — design never visibly changed the product, the experience or the business model. The board and the older co-designers see the gap, and so will the market.`,
                    insight_threevalue:     `Personal, business and societal value reinforced each other: the co-design that created dignity is the same co-design that created desirability and a defensible margin. You refused the trade-off — and that is the longevity-leadership lesson.`,

                    hint_reskin:            `You re-skinned a meaning problem. A new colour scored 8% last month — shift to true inclusive design and fund the €1.1M cliff, or the failure repeats.`,
                    hint_everyone:          `You’re serving both segments at once and satisfying neither. Commit to one identity-driven target and design the whole line around its job-to-be-done.`,
                    hint_payback:           `Payback is past the 30-month hurdle. Re-price toward the value you built (Active Agers will pay) and fund the channel — a line that can’t pay back gets cancelled.`,
                    hint_desire:            `Desirability is still below the 25% hurdle. Lead with dignity and desirability, not assistance — older buyers reject anything that makes them feel old.`,
                    hint_claim:             `Your plan says “design-led” but design hasn’t visibly changed the product. Make the design decision real in the product, the experience and the business model.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — desirability above the hurdle, personal and societal value both healthy. Lock in the inclusive design, defend the three-value balance, and win on meaning.`,

